  "description": "The ML.NET integration for OptunaSharp (a .NET port of Optuna): ML.NET-backed forest parameter-importance evaluators over the Microsoft.ML.FastTree runtime. A faithful FastForest backend — FastForestFanovaImportanceEvaluator (fANOVA — Hutter et al. 2014) and FastForestMeanDecreaseImpurityImportanceEvaluator (MDI) — plus EXPERIMENTAL / off-label gradient-boosted-tree backends (ML.NET FastTree and FastTreeTweedie): FastTree/FastTreeTweedie {Fanova,MeanDecreaseImpurity}ImportanceEvaluator. The canonical FanovaImportanceEvaluator / MeanDecreaseImpurityImportanceEvaluator ship in Core as a pure-managed, bit-exact port of scikit-learn's RandomForestRegressor (value-parity with Python Optuna); FastForest fits that same bagging model with ML.NET (ranking parity — values diverge, not tree-identical to scikit-learn), while the boosters fit the ensemble residual rather than the objective, so fANOVA/MDI over them is off-label — a non-standard quantity with NO value-parity to Optuna (weak ranking agreement at best). Prefer the canonical Core evaluators when Optuna parity matters. Also ships the ML.NET backend for the Core OptunaSearchCV (the Phase-6 optuna.integration analog): MLNetEstimator, the ICrossValidationEstimator that cross-validates an arbitrary ML.NET pipeline — an adapter (validated by usage, not bit-parity), since ML.NET is not scikit-learn. (OptunaSearchCV itself, the ICrossValidationEstimator seam, and the byte-exact native scikit-learn backend all live in Core — the latter under namespace OptunaSharp.Integration.Sklearn.) The framework-agnostic PruningCallback — for driving pruning from your own training loop — lives in Core (OptunaSharp) as the base that framework integration packages extend. LightGBM lives in the separate OptunaSharp.Integration.LightGbm package (distinct heavy native runtime + Optuna tuner identity). Part of the SciSharp STACK.",
  "iconFile": "optunasharp-icon.png",
